# @@NAME@@

R3 robot project. `robot.yaml` describes the robot once; the R3 pipeline
derives CAD, PCBs, 3D wiring and deployable software from it and validates the
result in a multi-domain digital twin.

## Quick start

```bash
python -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
pip install "r3-platform[sim,mcp] @ @@PLATFORM_URL@@@@@PLATFORM_REF@@"
r3 validate            # all stages, writes build/report/index.html
r3 diagnose            # errors grouped by component / wire / binding with hints
r3 sim run --scenario @@FIRST_SCENARIO@@
```

Open `build/report/index.html` for diagnostics, scenario results and plots.

## Agents

Codex and Claude Code read `AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md`. The `r3` MCP server is
registered in `.mcp.json` for Claude Code; for Codex add to `~/.codex/config.toml`:

```toml
[mcp_servers.r3]
command = "python"
args = ["-m", "r3_orchestrator.mcp_server"]
env = { R3_PROJECT = "/path/to/@@NAME@@" }
```

## CI

`.github/workflows/r3.yml` validates every push and pull request, uploads
`build/` as an artifact and posts a job summary. Optional secrets:
`ONSHAPE_ACCESS_KEY` / `ONSHAPE_SECRET_KEY` (CAD sync) and `R3_PLATFORM_TOKEN`
(private platform repository). KiCad steps run when `kicad-cli` is available.

## Deploy

`r3 deploy --target sim` runs the software against the twin.
`r3 deploy --target fleet --yes` deploys to the robots through BLITZ
(`backend/deploy.py`); it refuses unless the current `robot.yaml` validated
without errors. Install the BLITZ development backend into this project first
(see the BLITZ README) so `backend/deployment/` exists.
